WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:11.600
"People gotta understand. You gotta attack, attack, attack."

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.560
(Stream)

00:00:14.680 --> 00:00:18.360
"Certain individuals you don't attack. You run from."

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:25.440
"I'm talking if I see you, and believe me I will. I'll put you down quickly."

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:36.360
(Stream)

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.640
"Whatever game that I'm playing. Make sure you're not playing that game."

00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:45.880
"What Dr. DisRespect's talking about, is some pain deliverance."

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.600
"And if you don't wanna approach me."

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.240
(Stream)

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.800
"I'll be real serious with ya."

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:58.760
"If you wanna approach me. I will kill you immediately."

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:06.400
(Stream)

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:15.680
Guy “DrDisrespect” Beahm wasn’t always the two-time champ,

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.920
"I'm the two-time 1993 1994 blockbuster video game champion in the online gaming community."

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:23.560
the face of Twitch, and

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:30.320
a man with self-proclaimed venomous gaming athleticism who kinda resembles an 80s style

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:31.440
action star.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:43.480
He was born on March 10, 1982, and his life in video games started as, well

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:44.800
a joke.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:50.040
"My gamertag was diarrhea panic, and I was online with a few buddies and they have ridiculous names as well."

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.160
Guy was a Varsity basketball player in university,

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:59.520
who spent his free time dominating in Halo and other first-person shooter games. He birthed

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:07.120
his now-heralded alter-ego “DrDisrespect” while playing Halo 2 on Xbox with friends.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:11.720
(Stream)

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:18.080
Back then, game developers still believe proximity comms were a good idea, so if you were close

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:21.400
to an enemy you could actively taunt them as you killed them.

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.960
(Stream)

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:31.240
And it was in Halo 2 where that gravely, 80’s action-star voice was born. The doc started

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:37.200
his assault on the online gaming world by uploading short YouTube clips of him, well,

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.240
disrespecting his opponents.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:51.680
(Stream)

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.340
And  like any top content creator, it didn’t

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:58.000
hurt that he was absolutely lethal at the games he was playing.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:05.480
(Stream)

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:11.150
It was on Jan. 5, 2010 that he first started posting to YouTube in conjunction with his

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:12.560
college roommates.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:15.720
"Just as blessed to be around good friends that we always had a camera around us

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:18.480
you know we could just shoot at all times.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.260
I could just cut it up, throw it up on YouTube. Boom. We got something."

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:26.680
He’d generally post gameplay footage and perform voice-over in his doctor

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:27.380
persona.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:33.080
At the same time, he changed his Halo in-game username to “DrDisrespect” and that’s

00:03:33.080 --> 00:03:37.300
where the lines between Guy Beahm and the doc began to blur.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:41.860
"So I guess with the creation of that gamertag, came the creation of the character."

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:47.400
Perhaps maybe the most apt thing about his moniker is the word disrespect — because

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:52.320
that’s how he’s built his brand. The entire origin of his alter ego stemmed from him making

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:56.580
plays in-game in order to best embarrass his opponent.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:04:00.480
The doctor was about disrespect from the beginning.

00:04:00.560 --> 00:04:11.120
(Stream)

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:17.360
On Jan.11, 2010 DrDisrespect began to up his

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:21.640
content creation game by creating his own YouTube channel.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:27.640
"What Dr. DisRespect's talking about, is some pain deliverance.

00:04:27.640 --> 00:04:29.200
He was playing Call of Duty:

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:35.600
Modern Warfare 3 at the time, and built his content around, well, flaming his opponents.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:48.560
(Stream)

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:55.000
He’d also “collaborate” with other YouTube personalities with larger followings by calling

00:04:55.000 --> 00:04:56.800
them out and playing with them.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:03.740
"I reached out to Mr360Games. Said "Hey check out my video, if you don't I'd cut you up."

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:09.280
You know one of those type of emails. He actually viewed it, liked it, and did a shout out in one of his

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:14.160
videos. That sort of jump started my run."

00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:17.580
Dr.D immediately started to foster attention.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:22.940
His antics were the intensification of the way the world looked at the male gamer stereotype

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.440
of the time and his flame-first approach was pretty hilarious.

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:35.400
(Stream)

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.670
The Doc started creating content for a popular gaming channel called Machinima after his

00:05:40.670 --> 00:05:46.300
first wave of popularity and it was then when his following started to balloon for the first time.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:54.060
But, then, almost as quickly as his Youtube success began — DrDisrespect disappeared.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:06:03.940
On Feb.22, 2011, his YouTube channel and the followers of his impeccable venomous video

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:08.440
game athleticism were left without the content they were hungry for.

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:13.840
Guy Beahm put down his second life as the doctor to fulfill dreams of his own — a

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:16.400
career in the game design industry.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:22.200
"I didn't really know what was where I wanted to go. I just know I've always wanted to get into the

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:28.040
video game industry, the interactive entertainment industry. But like, I had no experience."

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:34.680
Beahm joined Sledgehammer Games on March. 16, 2011 — the game development company

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:39.360
responsible for multiple Call of Duty titles — as its community manager.

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:44.120
"I mean the only thing that really made sense to me, possibly become and community manager."

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:44.960
But even in

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:49.980
this new path in his life, it was the Doctor that built his name in the gaming community

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.640
to bolster his application at Sledgehammer.

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.600
"My experiences that I've gained in the creation of the Doctor character

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:01.680
from a social media perspective, in terms of growing the Twitter and the Facebook and how

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:06.640
to grow a YouTube channel, how to read feedback, engage with your fans your audience.

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:12.560
And at the same time, to be able to be involved in all of this, and understand video games.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:13.760
It just made sense."

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:18.800
After some time as community manager, Beahm was promoted to level designer for Call of

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:23.800
Duty: Advanced Warfare, where he participated in the development of seven of the maps in

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:24.600
the game.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.280
"I designed half the maps for Call of Duty: Advanced Warfare, multiplayer and also helped create a lot of the

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.500
high level pillars for the studio."

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:34.120
During his time at Sledgehammer guy created

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:39.120
a Justin.tv account and tried streaming a few times as the Doc.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:44.720
Now, as you might imagine, when you’ve built a personality as more or less a a second version

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:50.780
of yourself like Guy Beahm did with DrDisrespect, it’s kind of a challenge to keep that guy

00:07:50.780 --> 00:07:51.780
locked away.

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:59.080
So, in 2015 Guy Beahm quit his job at Sledgehammer, and unleashed Dr. Disrespect

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:00.940
"Hello!"

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:04.560
It began with some Youtube warnings about his return.

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:13.940
"I'm Dr. DisRespect, and as you know we've been away from the YouTube community for quite a while

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:20.240
now but, I'm here to tell you. I'm back, and I ain't messin around."

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:28.320
And then on March 21, 2016 — the man we’ve come to know as the Face of Twitch began streaming

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:29.080
full-time.

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:37.080
"The Doctor's been preparing himself, for his re entrance into the online gaming community."

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:42.720
Maybe more than any other streamer, Doc’s popularity skyrocketed with the development

00:08:42.740 --> 00:08:44.720
of the Battle Royale format.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:51.880
Doc was given early access to H1Z1, the zombie-themed Battle Royale that was the first of the genre

00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:56.990
to make headway. And it’s here where Doc’s love for BRs began.

00:08:56.990 --> 00:09:02.089
They were challenging, had enough down-time to support streaming and he could make his

00:09:02.089 --> 00:09:06.140
signature bad-mouthing a big part of his play-style.

00:09:06.220 --> 00:09:13.600
(Stream)

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:18.400
"Cause I like to remind these little chubby cheek wannabe unathletic pigeon toed sprinting gamers

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.520
what the two-time's all about."

00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:36.080
(Stream)

00:09:36.200 --> 00:09:41.500
While streaming H1Z1, Doc was averaging around 5- to 10 thousand viewers

00:09:41.500 --> 00:09:44.560
at a time and his stream was growing rapidly.

00:09:44.560 --> 00:09:50.660
But, if H1Z1 was the surprising-opener of DrDisrespect’s jumping into streaming celebration,

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:54.500
Player Unknown’s Battlegrounds was the main attraction.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:05.260
It’s hard to understate the impact that PUBG had on the streaming landscape. BRs,

00:10:05.260 --> 00:10:11.540
and specifically PUBG rewarded streamers with flashy highlights and clippable moments for

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:13.720
maximum social media content.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:20.400
And to that extent, PUBG and DrDisrespect couldn’t be a better match.

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:48.700
(Stream)

00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:51.260
During the summer of 2017, Doc’s

00:10:51.260 --> 00:10:57.220
average viewership reached as high as 30,000. He had acquired his nickname as “the face

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:03.060
of Twitch.” and received industry awards for both streamer of the year and gamer of the year.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:10.140
The internet, Twitch, gaming and DrDisrespect were now synonymous.

00:11:10.540 --> 00:11:17.580
"Let's climb the mountain, let's dominate. Violence, speed, and momentum. And guess what, we're at the

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:24.600
tippity top of the mountain. But we're really only half way up."

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:30.760
"Let's do it baby. Thank you. Thank you.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:36.059
He dawned a thick moustache and dollar store sunglasses — and people

00:11:36.060 --> 00:11:36.860
loved it.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:12:11.560
(Stream)

00:12:11.560 --> 00:12:16.560
But the one day, Guy Beahm broke character.

00:12:17.000 --> 00:12:22.240
"As you guys know I have a beautiful family. And a wife and kid.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:28.400
I want to be transparent that I've been unfaithful."

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:34.680
His admission was of course devoid of the pompous charisma of the doctor and featured

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:39.840
bashfulness far-removed from the usual attitude that people have come to expect from

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:40.960
the Twitch channel.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:45.040
"And I'm probably gonna be taking some time away. Time off."

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:51.880
So after his short frank admission of guilt. Dr. DisRespect left Twitch.

00:12:53.640 --> 00:12:54.760
"That's it."

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:03.000
Dr. DisRespect has spent a lot of his time, as Twitch's darling.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:08.520
but even at the height of his success he’s been the target of heavy scrutiny.

00:13:08.520 --> 00:13:14.880
In the same way that he acts as a mirror image of the archetypical gamer, he’s also a symbol

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.160
of some of gaming’s most criticized characteristics.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:24.320
His on-stream personality is hyper-masculine in nature, he’s been criticized for being

00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:30.540
racially insensitive on multiple occasions and called offensive on countless others.

00:13:30.540 --> 00:13:37.290
It’s often hard to get a read on The Doc, but despite his persona’s brash nature,

00:13:37.290 --> 00:13:44.149
he’s not devoid of flashes of empathy, kindness and the familial community-building we see

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:46.160
from many top streamers.

00:13:46.160 --> 00:13:52.040
"I've realized that I've provided an outlet for a lot of people that are dealing with a lot of hardships.

00:13:52.760 --> 00:13:57.000
And, it makes me proud."

00:13:59.680 --> 00:14:03.800
As 2018 began, more than a month had passed since the Doctor's

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:08.160
last stream and the Twitch’s hero was nowhere to be found.

00:14:08.480 --> 00:14:14.680
Late January brought signs of Doc’s return and rumblings around social media and small

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:18.760
YouTube teaser marked his return for early February.

00:14:19.920 --> 00:14:25.840
"Ladies and gentlemen, the Doc is coming back."

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:32.480
And then  on Feb. 5, 2018 — DrDisrespect returned.

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:38.880
"I haven't played in a long time, I literally just woke up out of the water bed.

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:42.720
press the start streaming button, and here we are."

00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:48.240
And he broke Twitch right in half, setting a new mark for concurrent viewership on an

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:53.760
individual’s Twitch channel at 388,000 viewers.

00:14:54.040 --> 00:15:01.120
"You're looking at the Face of Twitch. And ladies and gentlemen the arena is wide open!"

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:31.600
(Stream)

00:15:31.660 --> 00:15:34.660
During that hallowed February, the Doc was

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:41.840
at full capacity, Guy Beahm was a thing of the past. He had gimmicks, he had flash and

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:48.800
it was all on level 100. He averaged a career high of more than 50,000 viewers at a time.

00:15:48.800 --> 00:15:57.020
Since then he’s stayed steady around 20-25k. DrDisrespect has become an integral part of

00:15:57.029 --> 00:16:02.489
the modern day Twitch ecosystem. He’s joined the likes of Shroud and Chad who’ve stayed

00:16:02.489 --> 00:16:07.280
loyal to PUBG while the mainstream has shifted gears to Fortnite.

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:11.260
"I don't necessarily play it all the time I just haven't gotten into it enough yet but,

00:16:13.080 --> 00:16:19.900
"I'd hate to play a guy who's as good as me, or as equal as me. And he has the same weapon as me

00:16:19.900 --> 00:16:21.980
but guess what he has the gold weapon."

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:27.900
He’s a moniker for the old-guard of the video game world, and his loyalty and consistency has

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:29.680
produced a massive following.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:34.840
"You know I get excited when the Doc looks like he's ready to actually murder somebody."

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:38.280
(Stream)

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:41.540
His fame, impressive aim, ups and downs

00:16:41.540 --> 00:16:47.480
aside. One thing that’s under-appreciated about DrDisrespect is his ability to juggle

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:50.860
two personalities day in and day out.

00:16:51.640 --> 00:16:58.380
To his own admission, Guy Beahm and Dr Disrespect don’t share many personality traits.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:04.560
Guy is very literally morphing into the doctor when he goes live on Twitch every day.

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:09.720
"Do you have a difficulty transitioning between yourself and the Doc. Cause you're in character for so long."

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.860
"And for so intense amount of time."

00:17:12.760 --> 00:17:17.480
"Do you have trouble being like, okay now I'm just a guy, literally Guy"

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:23.320
"No, no we shut it down, we approach it as a business."

00:17:23.600 --> 00:17:28.580
When we tune into his stream every day, we are witnessing an impressively long piece

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.340
of performance art.

00:17:30.600 --> 00:17:36.880
That webcam flicks on, and it’s you and the screen and you better be entertaining,

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:44.880
(Stream)

00:17:45.360 --> 00:17:47.280
good at your game of choice

00:17:47.560 --> 00:17:49.800
(Stream)

00:17:49.800 --> 00:17:52.360
and handle it all with calm and collected.

00:17:52.640 --> 00:17:57.160
(Stream)

00:17:57.160 --> 00:18:04.630
At first glance DrDisrespect looks wild — just an aberration within the normal tame world

00:18:04.640 --> 00:18:05.600
of Twitch.

00:18:05.920 --> 00:18:08.320
"Ladies and gentlemen trickle in."

00:18:09.400 --> 00:19:00.000
(Stream)

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:05.120
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

